# REQUIRES: mlir-translate
#
# INVALID CALLABLE-WRAPPER SURVIVAL EXPERIMENT. This is a differential MLIR shape
# check, not an NFv2 release-carrier acceptance test and not a ModelStatus
# claim. NFv2 uses `llvm.sps.release` plus an external stable instruction
# binding; an outlined wrapper is structurally nonconforming even when pinned.
#
# The NF-A08 attribute set keeps a
# wrapper occurrence findable after the MLIR inliner runs. The same fixture is
# tested with and without those attributes so this compiler fact
# remains reproducible without granting the wrapper NFv2 status.
#
# The two arms differ ONLY in the passthrough attribute list. Everything else --
# the wrapper body, the caller, the store -- is byte-identical, so any difference
# in outcome is attributable to the attributes alone.
#
# RESULT THIS ENCODES. `mlir-opt --inline --cse` honours `passthrough
# ["noinline", ...]` on an `llvm.func`. The carrier survives when pinned and is
# spliced away when not. That is worth having as a regression, because the whole
# invalid wrapper-binding experiment depends on it. This is a property of the
# MLIR inliner rather than evidence for the current SPS carrier.

# RUN: not grep -F 'llvm.sps.release' %fixtures/release-carrier/pinned-control/release_carrier_pinned.control.mlir

# --- pinned arm: the call must survive -------------------------------------
# RUN: %mlir-opt %fixtures/release-carrier/pinned-control/release_carrier_pinned.control.mlir --inline --cse -o %t.pinned.mlir
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PINNED --input-file=%t.pinned.mlir

# PINNED-LABEL: llvm.func @release_carrier_pinned_control
# PINNED: llvm.call @sps_release_invalid_callable
# PINNED: llvm.store

# --- unpinned arm: the call must be gone ------------------------------------
# Strip only the pinning attributes; keep the module otherwise identical.
# RUN: sed 's/"noinline", "nomerge", "noduplicate", "nobuiltin"/"nounwind"/' %fixtures/release-carrier/pinned-control/release_carrier_pinned.control.mlir > %t.unpinned.mlir
# RUN: %mlir-opt %t.unpinned.mlir --inline --cse -o %t.unpinned.out.mlir
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=UNPINNED --input-file=%t.unpinned.out.mlir

# UNPINNED-LABEL: llvm.func @release_carrier_pinned_control
# UNPINNED-NOT: llvm.call @sps_release_invalid_callable
# UNPINNED: llvm.and
# UNPINNED: llvm.store

# INTERPRETATION, so this is not over-read.
#
# The unpinned arm is NOT a leak. Its arithmetic is identical and it publishes
# the same value. What it has lost is the ability to be BOUND: there is no direct
# call to the manifest-named wrapper, so no stable SiteId, no ABI-role mapping,
# and no call-occurrence cardinality to match against the manifest. The correct
# disposition is a refusal, not a counterexample. See
# fixtures/release-carrier/lost-bad/release_carrier_lost.bad.mlir.
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE. This shows only that the MLIR inliner honours the attribute.
# The wrapper is an NFv2 negative regardless of survival. Current intrinsic
# preservation is tested separately and is feature-gated until patched LLVM is
# available.
